WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Least privilege means code should have only the authority needed for the work it is doing now.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.800
In a C program, this is an architecture principle, not a slogan.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:15.260
The safest privileged operation is the one that is not part of the general processing path.

4
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:24.480
If most parsing, networking, formatting, business logic, and user-driven behavior can run with ordinary rights, the design should make that the normal case.

5
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.920
A common pattern is to separate privileged setup from ordinary processing.

6
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:39.180
The program may need authority to open a protected resource, bind a port, prepare a directory, read a trusted configuration file, or initialize a controlled resource.

7
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:46.640
After that setup, the remaining logic should be designed to run with reduced authority where the platform and application model allow it.

8
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.760
Keeping privileged sections small has practical benefits.

9
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:57.500
Smaller sections are easier to review, easier to test, and easier to explain to operations teams.

10
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.400
They also make it clearer which inputs must be validated before authority is used.

11
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:16.580
A broad helper function that reads configuration, validates user input, opens files, spawns children, updates state, and logs decisions is difficult to reason about because its authority is hidden in a large surface.

12
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.860
Least privilege also applies to files and resources.

13
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.800
A program should not need write access to a whole directory tree when it only appends to one controlled file.

14
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.780
A service account should not need broad database rights when it only reads a narrow set of records.

15
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.880
A child process should not inherit handles or descriptors that have no purpose in the child.

16
00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.140
The design should remove unnecessary access before code depends on it.

17
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:48.740
For maintainability, draw the boundary in the documentation and in the structure of the code.

18
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.640
Name the privileged setup functions clearly.

19
00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.820
Keep policy checks near the operations they authorize.

20
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.120
Add tests that fail when authority is broader than expected.

21
00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:05.940
Least privilege works best when future maintainers can see the intended boundary without reconstructing it from scattered side effects.
